/*
# The Declare homepage

The page you are reading, written in Declare. The design tokens live once as
semantic type components, and the page is a vertical **flow** of auto-sizing
sections — no absolute-y. Each section's height falls out of its content, so
the page stacks and re-flows on its own at any width. The full-bleed chrome —
the blueprint grid, the sticky header, the pointer dot — sits absolutely
around the centered column.

`{ }` bodies are TypeScript, so colors there are `0x…`; `[ ]` literal slots
take `#…`. Every color on the page has a name in one of the two `theme`
records below — the palette lives there, once, and nowhere else.
*/

// what the site's programs share — the text faces, for now

include [ "fonts.declare" ]

/*
## The figures under an app card

`stats.json` is generated by the prewarm pass from the real production builds
(line metrics + the gzipped wire size), so these helpers are the only place the
page decides how to *say* a figure — never what it is. A missing value yields
"", so a card that outruns the stats file degrades to no figures rather than to
"undefined".

Each figure arrives as its OWN argument, because a helper's arguments are its
reactive wiring. Hand it the whole record instead and the reads happen inside
the body, where the dependency extractor cannot see them — the compiler refuses
that (DECLARE7001), and it is right to: the line would render once against an
unloaded DataSource and never update again.
*/

script {
    function hpLines(total: number, code: number, comment: number): string {
        if (total == null) return ""
        return total + " lines — " + code + " code, " + comment + " comment"
        }

    function hpWire(wire: number): string {
        return wire == null ? "" : " · " + Math.round(wire / 1024) + " KB over the wire"
        }
    }

/*
## Type as tokens

Every text style on the page is one of these classes — headings semibold with
tracking that scales with the size, eyebrows mono and wide-tracked, lead and
body in the muted tone — so the page's whole typography reskins from here.

None of them declares `selectable`. It is a PROVIDED value: the three content
columns (the front page, the Why article, a DocView) each say once that they
are copy, and every text leaf beneath reads the nearest provision. A class
that repeated it said the same thing five times — and said it about the
wordmark and a tuner label too, which are chrome. What is NOT copy says so at
the control: `selectable = false` on a link, a button, a panel chip.
*/

class Heading extends Text [ fontWeight = { app.headWeight }, textColor = { provided("theme").text },
    letterSpacing = { app.headTrack * fontSize }
    ]


// The same treatment, worn by things that are NOT headings — a sequence
// numeral, a stat figure, the wordmark. Identical to `Heading` in every pixel;
// what it carries is intent. `Heading` sets weight, colour and tracking but no
// size, so on its own it says "display type" rather than "a heading", and the
// static extractor reads a document outline off the settled type. Saying which
// of the two a piece of text is keeps the outline honest at the source.

class Display extends Heading [ ]

class Eyebrow extends Text [ fontFamily = ["ui-monospace", "monospace"], fontSize = 13,
    fontWeight = semibold, letterSpacing = 1.6, textColor = { provided("theme").muted }
    ]


// the accent section number

class Marker extends Eyebrow [ textColor = { provided("theme").accent } ]

// footer text in the Declare typeface (App Sans), not the mono eyebrow

class FootLabel extends Text [ fontSize = 13, fontWeight = semibold, textColor = { provided("theme").muted } ]

class Lead extends Text [ fontSize = 19, fontWeight = normal, textColor = { provided("theme").lead } ]

class Body extends Text [ fontSize = 17, fontWeight = normal, textColor = { provided("theme").muted } ]

// The CENTERED band treatment — the two closing sections (the feedback loop
// and the closer) are statements addressed to the whole column rather than
// copy running down its left edge, so each line is centered on its own
// measure. `measure` is the only thing that differs between the four of them,
// and `x = center` with `textAlign = center` is one idea, so it is one class
// rather than two attributes to keep in step at every use site.

class BannerHead extends Heading [ measure: number = 880,
    width = { Math.min(this.measure, parent.width) }, x = center, textAlign = center
    ]


class BannerLead extends Lead [ measure: number = 620,
    width = { Math.min(this.measure, parent.width) }, x = center, textAlign = center
    ]


// the short accent rule the closer wears above and below its line

class BandRule extends View [ x = center, width = 140, height = 2, fill = { provided("theme").accent } ]

// The site's prose lives beside this program, not inside it: why.md, the
// getting-started guide, the FAQ and the language document are all fetched as
// text and flowed by the Markdown component, so every article on the site is
// one rendition with one set of numbers.


/*
## The header pills

A nav link is a padded pill: it fills with surface-2 on hover (a rounded rect
behind the text, not just a color change), and `pressed` deepens the pill and
lights the label — the tap feedback a touch device gets in place of hover.
The 12px each side is the view's own `padding`, so the label starts at the
content origin and the pill's width is simply what it holds.
*/

class NavLink extends View [ height = 33, cornerRadius = 8, padding = [0, 12, 0, 12],
    label:   string = "",

    // a pill WITH children opens the dropdown instead of following a link —
    // the wide rendition of the sheet's group rows. The anchor is snapshotted
    // at the gesture (rootOrigin, converted to viewport space), the sanctioned
    // overlay pattern (Menu.openAt).

    menu:    boolean = false,
    onClick() {
        if (!this.menu) return
        const o = this.rootOrigin()
        app.appsX = o.x - app.scrollX
        app.appsY = o.y - app.scrollY + this.height + 6
        app.appsOpen = !app.appsOpen
        },

    // ONE reference does all three jobs (location.md §0.3): "#why" is a
    // destination, "#apps" a bare anchor (the runtime derives its location and
    // reveals it), a URL leaves the app. The follow,
    // the real <a> (hover preview, ⌘-click, copy-link), the keyboard stop,
    // and the crawler's edge all come from the `link` the use site sets.

    // pressed deepens the pill + lights the label — the tap feedback a touch
    // device gets in place of hover (mouseDown/Up fire on a tap, hover does not).

    fill = { pressed ? provided("theme").pillPress : hovered ? provided("theme").pillHover : null },
    lbl: Text [ y = 8, fontFamily = { app.uiFace }, fontSize = 14,
        text = { classroot.label },
        textColor = { classroot.hovered || classroot.pressed ? provided("theme").text : provided("theme").muted } ],
    width = { this.contentWidth }
    ]


/*
## The mobile nav sheet

At phone width the pill row would ride the wordmark, so it collapses to a kebab
(the page's dot motif) that opens this sheet: a thumb-height row per destination,
drawn from the same `navItems` as the pills. Same press feedback as a pill.

The sheet is the one surface that can afford STRUCTURE. The pill row is bounded
by the window's width, so a destination with children shows there as a single
pill; the sheet is already open by the time it is read and vertical space is
free, so the children get their own rows under the parent — no second tap to
reach the thing you came for. One `navItems` list, two renditions, which is
what these two surfaces already were.
*/

class MobileNavRow extends View [ width = 100%, height = 44, cornerRadius = 8,
    label:   string = "",

    // a child row: same type size and the same thumb height as its parent —
    // these are destinations, not annotations, and shrinking them would say
    // otherwise. The INDENT alone carries the hierarchy, with the quieter
    // tone behind it.

    sub:     boolean = false,

    fill = { pressed ? provided("theme").pillPress : hovered ? provided("theme").pillHover : null },

    // the coexistence rule (location.md §0.4): the handler runs — closing the
    // sheet — then the follow. Navigation itself is the `link`.

    onClick()     { app.menuOpen = false; app.appsOpen = false },

    lbl: Text [ x = { classroot.sub ? 30 : 14 }, y = center, fontFamily = { app.uiFace },
        fontSize = 16, fontWeight = medium,
        text = { classroot.label },
        textColor = { classroot.hovered || classroot.pressed ? provided("theme").text : classroot.sub ? provided("theme").muted : provided("theme").lead } ]
    ]


// one navItems entry as it appears in the sheet: its own row, then a row per
// child. `:children[]` on an entry whose list is empty resolves to no records,
// so a childless destination is simply its own row — there is no branch here
// and no second rendition to keep in step.

class MobileNavGroup extends View [ width = 100%,
    height = { this.contentHeight },
    layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 0 ],
    MobileNavRow [ label = { :label }, link = { :link } ],
    MobileNavRow [ datapath = :children[], sub = true, label = { :label }, link = { :link } ]
    ]


/*
## Section furniture

The repeated shapes the numbered sections are built from: an `AppCard` (§01 —
a shot of a real application over its one-line description), a `MiniMetric`
figure, a `Point` with its accent top-rule, and the footer’s separator `Dot`.
*/

/*
### A screenshot frame

A clipped, rounded plate holding one app’s picture. Its height derives from its
OWN width at the shots’ 1.6 aspect, so whatever sizes it — a responsive share,
a layout, a card — gets the right box with no second number to keep in sync.
The use site declares the `link` that runs the app — View's own attribute, so
the plate gets a real anchor (hover preview, ⌘-click, copy-link) and the
crawler gets the edge; the stroke lifts to the accent rule on hover so the
plate reads as a door rather than a picture.
*/

class Shot extends View [
    src: string = "",

    // An optional MOVING version. `clip` is taken — it is View's own attribute —
    // so the moving source is `motion`, and the still becomes its poster:
    // `pic` shows until the clip has a frame, which is the composition a
    // `poster` attribute would have hard-coded. `live` gates playback, so a
    // clip below the fold is not decoding while nobody is looking.

    motion: string = "",
    live: boolean = true,

    // an outside hover — the card's own title — lights the plate too, so the
    // picture and the words behave as the single target they are

    hot: boolean = false,

    cornerRadius = 12, clip = true, fill = { provided("theme").stage },
    height = { this.width / 1.6 },

    // A GLOW, not a drop shadow. On a near-black ground a black shadow has
    // nothing to darken — it reads as almost nothing. A light halo does the
    // same job in the other direction: zero offset so it sits evenly around the
    // plate, and a low alpha so it separates the shot from the page without
    // announcing itself. The crisp edge is the `edge` overlay below; this is
    // the separation behind it.

    // colours here are RRGGBBAA (the theme's own spelling — `scrim` is
    // 0x0B141BB3), so the alpha is the LOW byte: a cool off-white at 0x4D
    // resting, 0x8C when the card is hot.

    shadow = { app.isDark ? shadow(0, 0, 26, hovered || hot ? 0xBFD8EA8C : 0xBFD8EA4D) : shadow(0, hovered || hot ? 12 : 6, hovered || hot ? 30 : 20, hovered || hot ? 0x14202A40 : 0x14202A2E) },

    pic: Image [ width = 100%, height = 100%,
        stretches = both, source = { classroot.src },
        visible = { classroot.motion == "" || !this.parent.reel.loaded } ],

    reel: Video [ visible = { classroot.motion != "" },
        width = 100%, height = 100%, stretches = both,
        loop = true, source = { classroot.motion },
        playing = { classroot.live && classroot.motion != "" } ],

    // THE EDGE, drawn OVER the picture — a stroke on the plate itself sits
    // under its children, so the screenshot or video painted over it and the
    // rule showed only where a picture left the edge bare. Translucent ink
    // (`plateEdge`) reads on a light picture and a dark one alike; the accent
    // rule on hover says "this is a door". It takes no pointer, so hovering
    // and pressing still land on the plate.

    edge: View [ width = 100%, height = 100%, cornerRadius = 12,
        pointerEvents = "none",
        stroke = { stroke(1, classroot.hovered || classroot.hot ? provided("theme").pointRule : provided("theme").plateEdge) } ]
    ]


/*
### A small navigating link

The page’s quiet link rendition — the accent, brightening on hover and flashing
at the press, the same direction every other text link here answers a tap. Not
selectable: it is a control, not copy. Where it goes is View's own `link`, set
at the use site, so it is a real anchor and a crawlable edge.
*/

class SmallLink extends Text [ fontFamily = { app.uiFace },
    fontSize = 14, fontWeight = medium, wrap = false, selectable = false,
    textColor = { pressed ? provided("theme").linkPress : hovered ? provided("theme").linkHover : provided("theme").accent }
    ]


/*
### A compact figure

A number over its note, sized to stay three-up in the space it is given — the
calendar's row of figures sits inside a half-column, where a band-width figure
that goes full-width on a narrow page would stack the three into a tower. The
40 is the two 20px gaps its row's layout puts between them.
*/

class MiniMetric extends View [
    value: string = "",
    note:  string = "",
    width = { (parent.width - 40) / 3 },
    layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 4 ],
    Display [ fontSize = 34, width = 100%, textFill = { provided("theme").brand },
        text = { classroot.value } ],
    Body [ fontSize = 12, width = 100%, text = { classroot.note } ]
    ]


/*
### The sequence badge

The three applications are the page’s one enumerated set, and the layout around
them is busy enough that the set reads as three unrelated rows. A number pins
each title to the sequence: the ordinal AT THE TITLE’S OWN SIZE — a `Heading`,
so font, weight and tracking are the title’s exactly, and the baselines match
by construction (same metrics, boxes top-aligned in the same row) — in the
page’s ink on a plain disc, white on the dark page and dark on paper. Plain,
not accent: the discs must read as counters against the noise, not as three
more accent-colored controls.
*/

class AppNum extends View [ width = 30,
    n: string = "",

    // the row's hover fact, bound in by the use site — the disc answers the
    // SAME state the title answers, so the pair lights as one target

    hot: boolean = false,
    visible = { n != "" },

    // BASELINE BY CONSTRUCTION, because there is no metrics read to do it by
    // arithmetic: the numeral is a `Heading` at the title's own size, so it
    // shares the title's font, weight and tracking exactly — and this box's
    // height IS the numeral's line box, top-aligned in the head row beside the
    // title's. Same metrics, same box top, same baseline, no font constants.

    height = { this.num.height },

    // the disc paints first (under the digit): white at rest, the accent while
    // the row is hot or the badge itself is pressed — the title's own change,
    // answered in fill rather than ink. The NUMERAL stays ink in every state:
    // ink-on-accent is already the page's text-on-accent pairing (Button).
    // Centered on the line box — a disc has no baseline of its own, so the box
    // center is the honest anchor.

    disc: View [ width = 30, height = 30, cornerRadius = 15,
        fill = { classroot.hot || classroot.pressed ? provided("theme").accent : provided("theme").badge },
        y = center ],
    num: Display [ fontSize = 22, wrap = false, selectable = false, textColor = { provided("theme").ink },
        x = center,
        text = { classroot.n } ]
    ]


/*
### An application card

The tracker and the desktop: a screenshot over the app’s name, what it does as
one dot-separated run, its figures small and dim, and a source link. The plate
and the name run the app; the source link opens the reader — and because events
do not bubble, that link answers its own click without the card also firing.
*/

/*
### An application's title row

The badge, the name and (where there is one) the arrow, all three linked to the
same place and all three answering one hover fact. Both application rows wear it, and
because it is a class each part reads the row's hover state as `classroot.hot`
— the depth it sits at is not part of the arithmetic.
*/

class CardHead extends View [ width = 100%, height = { this.contentHeight },
    num:   string = "",
    title: string = "",
    url:   string = "",
    hot:   boolean = false,
    arrow: boolean = false,
    layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = x, spacing = 10 ],

    seq: AppNum [ n = { classroot.num }, hot = { classroot.hot }, link = { classroot.url } ],
    name: Heading [ fontSize = 22, wrap = false, selectable = false,
        textColor = { classroot.hot ? provided("theme").accent : provided("theme").text },
        text = { classroot.title },
        link = { classroot.url } ],

    // the arrow carries the reference only when it is shown: a hidden view with
    // a `link` is still a real anchor, and a row that has no arrow should not
    // leave a focusable, crawlable one behind it

    arr: Text [ visible = { classroot.arrow }, y = 3, fontSize = 16, fontWeight = semibold,
        wrap = false, selectable = false,
        textColor = { classroot.hot ? provided("theme").accent : provided("theme").dim }, text = "→",
        link = { classroot.arrow ? classroot.url : "" } ]
    ]


class AppCard extends View [
    title:   string = "",
    num:     string = "",
    shot:    string = "",
    blurb:   string = "",
    figures: string = "",
    url:     string = "",
    srcUrl:  string = "",
    layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 16 ],

    // TWO hover facts, not one. `titleHot` is the words; `hot` is the words or
    // the plate. The plate reads `titleHot` and the words read `hot`, so a
    // pointer anywhere on either lights BOTH — and there is no cycle, because
    // each derives from the raw `hovered` of the other, never from its styling.

    titleHot: boolean = { this.head.seq.hovered || this.head.name.hovered || this.head.arr.hovered },
    hot: boolean = { this.frame.hovered || this.titleHot },

    // the name LEADS: you read what the thing is, then look at it. The plate
    // under a title also gives the row a left edge to hang from, which three
    // pictures of different interiors do not.

    head: CardHead [ arrow = true, num = { classroot.num }, title = { classroot.title },
        url = { classroot.url }, hot = { classroot.hot } ],

    frame: Shot [ width = 100%, src = { classroot.shot }, link = { classroot.url },
        hot = { classroot.titleHot } ],

    copy: View [ width = 100%,
        layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 10 ],
        say: Body [ fontSize = 15, width = 100%, text = { classroot.blurb } ],
        fig: Body [ fontSize = 13, width = 100%, textColor = { provided("theme").dim },
            text = { classroot.figures } ],
        src: SmallLink [ text = "View & edit source →", link = { classroot.srcUrl } ]
        ]
    ]


// The accent rule is the box's own TOP BORDER — one side of a four-sided
// `stroke`. A stroke is drawn inside the box and takes no room, so the 18px
// above the title is the box's own `padding`, and the title and body are
// simply the two things a Point holds. (A per-side stroke tapers into a
// rounded corner rather than mitring; this box has square corners.)

class Point extends View [ width = { Math.min(357, parent.width) },
    ptitle: string = "",
    body:   string = "",
    stroke = { [ stroke(2, provided("theme").pointRule), null, null, null ] },
    padding = [18, 0, 0, 0],
    layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 8 ],
    Heading [ fontSize = 17, text = { classroot.ptitle } ],
    Body    [ fontSize = 15, width = 100%, text = { classroot.body } ]
    ]


class Dot extends Body [ fontSize = 14, textColor = { provided("theme").faint }, text = "·" ]

/*
## Buttons

The site's two calls to action. They extend the library's `Button`, so focus,
the focus ring, Space and Enter arrive with the control — the way to get a
particular look is to subclass the control, not to rebuild it.

The subclass restates only the page's own skin: the taller box, the softer
corner, and the interaction shades. The library's primary fill is flat by house
style; every other target on this page brightens on hover and deepens at the
press, and the main call to action should not be the one exception.
*/

class SiteButton extends Button [ height = 46, cornerRadius = 9,
    fill = { primary ? (down ? provided("theme").accentPress : hot ? provided("theme").accentHover : provided("theme").accent)
                     : (down ? provided("theme").press       : hot ? provided("theme").hover       : provided("theme").surface) },
    stroke = { primary ? null : stroke(1, down ? provided("theme").accentSecondary : provided("theme").line) },

    // the one ink decision, the library's own seam: the page's ground colour
    // reads on the accent, the ordinary text colour on the quiet fill

    inkColor = { primary ? provided("theme").ink : provided("theme").text }
    ]


/*
## The site footer

One line, shared by every page of the site — the scrolling front page and the
in-app Why / Language / Get Started / FAQ views alike.

A `FootLink` is the footer’s link rendition: the eyebrow treatment with the
teal hover and the brighter pressed tap feedback, over View's own `link`.
*/

class FootLink extends FootLabel [

    // A navigating link is a control, not copy — the content column's
    // provision is for the static footer text and stops at this one.

    selectable = false,
    textColor = { pressed ? provided("theme").accentSecondaryPress : hovered ? provided("theme").accentSecondary : provided("theme").muted }
    ]


// the footer separator: a faint dot that drops out once the row wraps, so no dot
// is ever stranded at a line's end (the 620 line is where the row stops fitting)

class FootSep extends FootLabel [ text = "·", textColor = { provided("theme").faint }, visible = { parent.width >= 620 } ]

// One centered flow — every item on a line, dot-separated at full width. As the
// width narrows the separator dots drop out and the items wrap to centered rows
// (the wider spacing keeps them legible), so provenance and legal never collide
// the way a left/right split does. Dropping the dots on wrap is what avoids a
// stranded separator at a line's end.
//
// The bottom margin is the footer's own `padding`, not a trailing spacer in the
// page flow: a spacer would take the flow's section spacing above it too, and
// that left a large dead band. With the inset counted into the unset height,
// the box is exactly the row plus the air under it.

class Footer extends View [ width = 100%,
    padding = [0, 0, 36, 0],
    layout: WrappingLayout [ justify = center, spacing = { parent.width >= 620 ? 10 : 18 }, rowSpacing = 10 ],

    // "Built in Declare" is one text RUN (HTMLText carries the brand accent
    // inline), so it keeps a word space, not a gap

    built: HTMLText [ width = 108, fontSize = 13, fontWeight = semibold, bodyColor = { provided("theme").muted },
        textStyles = { { g: { textFill: provided("theme").brand } } },
        html = "Built in <span class='g'>Declare</span>" ],
    FootSep [ ],
    FootLink [ link = "apps/homepage/homepage.declare?viewer", text = "View Source" ],
    FootSep [ ],
    FootLink [ link = "apps/docs/docs.declare", text = "Docs" ],
    FootSep [ ],
    FootLink [ link = "https://github.com/davidtemkin/declarelang", text = "GitHub" ],
    FootSep [ ],
    lic: FootLink [ link = "https://github.com/davidtemkin/declarelang/blob/main/LICENSE", text = "MIT License" ],
    FootSep [ ],
    FootLabel [ text = "© 2026 David Temkin" ]
    ]


/*
## The live panels

The heart of the page: every demo is real Declare, compiled and running. A
`CodeField` is a native editable field seeded (uncontrolled, `initial`) from
the demo’s source — it follows the source until you type, then holds the
edit, publishing each keystroke on `App.liveSource` for the host to
recompile. A `SourcePanel` frames it (LIVE·EDITABLE chip, Revert, hairline);
a `PreviewFrame` hosts the compiled app as an **embedded child app** — no
iframe — so the preview can’t drift from the source.
*/

class CodeField extends TextInput [ multiline = true, spellcheck = false,
    fontFamily = ["ui-monospace", "monospace"], fontSize = 11, textColor = { provided("theme").lead },
    editName: string = "",
    src: string = "",
    initial = { src },
    onInput() { app.liveCard = editName; app.liveSource = text }
    ]


// Neither the field nor the panel carries a height: a field with none sizes to
// its text, and the panel sizes to the field. So the code is never a scrolling
// listing — it re-fits as the column re-flows, and as you type in it. The
// preview beside it takes the same height. Revert restores the pristine `src`
// and asks the host to recompile.

class SourcePanel extends View [
    editName: string = "",
    src: string = "",
    fill = { provided("theme").panel }, cornerRadius = 12, stroke = { stroke(1, provided("theme").panelLine) },

    // A control surface inside the copy column, so the column's provision
    // stops at this box: the chip, the Revert label and the compile report are
    // furniture, and the code itself is a native editable field whose
    // selection is the platform's, not this flag's.

    selectable = false,

    // the panel is its field, plus the chip row above it and a margin below —
    // and, while a broken edit has a report, that report's band: it lands
    // UNDER the code (its own y is parent.height − its height), never over the
    // closing lines, because the page invites you to break the panel and read

    height = { this.field.y + this.field.height + 8 + (this.err.visible ? this.err.height : 0) },

    // the affordance chip: this code runs, and you can type in it. Both chips
    // are a label in a `padding` — the label sits at the content origin and the
    // chip's width is simply what it holds, so the inset is stated once.

    live: View [ x = 12, y = 7, height = 18, cornerRadius = 5, fill = { provided("theme").accent },
        padding = [4, 7, 4, 7],
        width = { this.contentWidth },
        t: Text [ fontFamily = { app.uiFace }, fontSize = 9, fontWeight = bold,
            letterSpacing = 0.8, wrap = false, textColor = { provided("theme").ink }, text = "LIVE · EDITABLE" ]
        ],
    revert: View [ y = 5, height = 20, cornerRadius = 6, stroke = { stroke(1, provided("theme").revertLine) },
        padding = [3, 8, 3, 8],
        x = { parent.width - this.width - 10 },
        width = { this.contentWidth },
        fill = { hovered ? provided("theme").hover : null },
        onClick() { classroot.field.text = classroot.src
                    app.liveCard = classroot.editName
                    app.liveSource = classroot.src },
        rlbl: Text [ fontFamily = { app.uiFace }, fontSize = 11, fontWeight = semibold,
            textColor = { parent.hovered ? provided("theme").text : provided("theme").lead }, text = "Revert" ]
        ],

    // The panel itself takes no `padding`: the hairline and the error band are
    // deliberately full-bleed — a rule that stopped short of the panel's edge
    // would read as a mistake — so an inset here would have to be undone by
    // both of them. The two children that ARE inset say so themselves.

    hairline: Divider [ y = 31, color = { provided("theme").panelLine } ],
    field: CodeField [ x = 12, y = 40, width = { parent.width - 24 },
        editName = { classroot.editName }, src = { classroot.src } ],

    // the compile report, at the panel — a broken edit shows its error right here
    // (the last good preview keeps running beside it); host feeds app.liveReport

    err: View [ width = 100%, fill = { provided("theme").errBg },
        visible = { app.liveCard == classroot.editName && app.liveReport != "" },
        y = { parent.height - this.height },
        height = { this.msg.height + 16 },
        bar: View [ width = 3, height = 100%, fill = { provided("theme").errBar } ],
        msg: Text [ x = 14, y = 8, width = { parent.width - 28 }, wrap = true,
            fontFamily = ["ui-monospace", "monospace"], fontSize = 11, textColor = { provided("theme").errText },
            text = { app.liveReport } ]
        ]
    ]


// the running demo is transparent: it sits on the stage colour, which follows
// the page, and draws only in colours that read on either ground

class PreviewFrame extends View [ cornerRadius = 12, clip = true, fill = { provided("theme").stage },
    stroke = { stroke(1, provided("theme").line) },
    runName: string = "",
    run: AppIsland [ width = 100%, height = 100%, program = { classroot.runName } ]
    ]


/*
### A live demo section

Sections 02–04 share one shape — a `SectionHead`, a caption, and a live
`SourcePanel` + `PreviewFrame` pair running the demo for real — so they are one
class, named for that role, and the three use sites are the words that differ.
`demo` is the demo's name in `app.demoSources`, the host→app seed channel: it
picks the source the editor starts from AND the program the island runs, which
is exactly the pairing that must never drift.

The two panels share the row 58/42 and stack below 760; the preview takes the
source panel's height, so the code is never a scrolling listing and the picture
beside it is always the same size as the code that makes it.
*/

class DemoSection extends View [ width = 100%,
    num:     string = "",
    head:    string = "",
    lead:    string = "",
    caption: string = "",
    demo:    string = "",
    layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 36 ],
    SectionHead [ num = { classroot.num }, head = { classroot.head }, lead = { classroot.lead } ],
    demoBody: View [ width = 100%,
        layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 14 ],
        Body [ fontSize = 15, width = 100%, text = { classroot.caption } ],
        cards: View [ width = 100%,
            layout: ResponsiveLayout [ plan = { [({ from: 760, flow: "row", gap: 20, share: ({ src: 58, prev: 42 }) }), ({ from: 0, flow: "stack", gap: 20, share: ({ src: 100, prev: 100 }) })] } ],
            src: SourcePanel [ editName = { classroot.demo },
                src = { app.demoSources[classroot.demo] || "" } ],
            prev: PreviewFrame [ runName = { classroot.demo }, height = { parent.src.height } ]
            ]
        ]
    ]


/*
## Backdrop and section heads

The blueprint grid draws itself; a `SectionHead` is the tight
number · heading · lead rhythm every numbered section opens with.
*/

/*
The blueprint backdrop is VIEWPORT-sized and pinned, never document-sized.

`draw()` rasterises into a canvas the size of the view's bounds at device pixel
ratio, so a backdrop stretched over a whole document would allocate a backing
store for the whole document: the language view is 16,711px tall, which at 2x
is a 2882 x 33,422 canvas — 96 megapixels, ~385 MB, reallocated every time the
view is shown or hidden, and about a second of blocked main thread each way.
It would also exceed Chrome's 32,767px maximum canvas dimension. Viewport-sized
and pinned, the same two moments measure 57 ms and 21 ms.

A uniform 32px lattice looks the same pinned as it does scrolling, so this
costs nothing visually — it is the same paper, drawn once.

Every view on the site that wears the paper wears the SAME paper, so the
geometry is the class's and a use site says only one thing: `bottom`, the
content's own extent it must not run past. It follows the scroll rather than
spanning the document — `ignoreScroll` would lift it out of paint order and
composite it OVER the content; as an ordinary child it stays behind. Snapped to
the 32px lattice so the paper is pixel-identical to the document-tall version —
the same lines in the same places — while only one viewport is ever rasterised.

The clamp is the part that has to be right. Unclamped, the backdrop's bottom is
`scrollY` + a viewport, which feeds the scroller's auto-extent — so every trip
to the bottom grew the page another 32px and left dead space under the footer
(measured: 5243 → 5412 over six trips). Reading the content's extent is what the
document-tall version did too, and it is not a cycle: the content never reads
the backdrop.
*/

class GridBg extends View [

    // the bottom of the content this paper backs, in the same space as this
    // view's own y — the one number a use site supplies

    bottom: number = 0,

    width = 100%,
    height = { app.height + 32 },
    y = { Math.max(0, Math.min(Math.floor(app.scrollY / 32) * 32, this.bottom - this.height)) },

    draw(d: Draw) {
        d.fillStyle = provided("theme").grid
        for (let gx = 0; gx <= width;  gx += 32) d.fillRect(gx, 0, 1, height)
        for (let gy = 0; gy <= height; gy += 32) d.fillRect(0, gy, width, 1)
        }
    ]


class SectionHead extends View [ width = 100%,
    num:  string = "",
    head: string = "",
    lead: string = "",
    layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 14 ],
    Marker  [ text = { classroot.num } ],
    Heading [ fontSize = 40, width = { Math.min(900, parent.width) }, text = { classroot.head } ],
    Lead    [ width = { Math.min(760, parent.width) }, text = { classroot.lead } ]
    ]


/*
### A band that fades in as it arrives

The reveal is a reactive TARGET, not a schedule: the spring's `to` is 1 once
the viewport's bottom edge has passed 90px into the band, and 0 before — so it
plays on the way down, unplays on the way back up, and is interruptible in
both directions, with nothing to start or cancel.

`classroot.parent.y + classroot.y` is the band's top in the SCROLLER's space —
its own y inside the content column, plus the column's own y — which is the
space `app.scrollY` is measured in. Reading it from `classroot` rather than
counting `parent` hops upward from inside the Spring is what lets one class
serve every use site: the arithmetic no longer depends on how deep the spring
happens to sit.
*/

class ScrollReveal extends View [ width = 100%, opacity = 0,
    reveal: Spring [ attribute = opacity, stiffness = 90, damping = 22, epsilon = 0.004,
        to = { app.scrollY + app.height > classroot.parent.y + classroot.y + 90 ? 1 : 0 } ]
    ]


/*
### A rendered document view

The Language doc, the getting-started guide and the FAQ are ONE view with three
sources: the site's paper, the centered 720 measure, an eyebrow, and a
`Markdown` fed from a `DataSource` — with loading and failure as states the view
derives from, never a blank. A use site says which document, in which words; the
shape lives here once, so the three cannot drift apart.

Each lifecycle fact arrives as its OWN argument, the same discipline the stats
helpers at the top of this file follow: an argument is a helper's reactive
wiring, and a `DataSource` handed over whole would move the reads inside a body
where the dependency extractor cannot see them.

The air under the footer is the column's own bottom `padding` rather than a tail
view in the flow: an unset height counts both insets, so the column is exactly
its content plus the room under it, and the page's scroll extent follows.
*/

class DocView extends View [ width = { app.width }, fill = { provided("theme").ink },
    eyebrow: string = "",
    waiting: string = "",
    trouble: string = "",
    loading: boolean = false,
    failed:  boolean = false,
    loaded:  boolean = false,
    reason:  string = "",
    markdown: string = "",

    grid: GridBg [ bottom = { parent.body.y + parent.body.height } ],
    body: View [ y = 56, width = { app.width }, selectable = true,
        col: View [ width = { Math.min(720, app.width - 48) }, x = center, y = 52,
            padding = [0, 0, 62, 0],
            layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 22 ],
            Marker [ text = { classroot.eyebrow } ],
            wait: Text [ visible = { classroot.loading }, fontSize = 15,
                textColor = { provided("theme").faint }, text = { classroot.waiting } ],
            oops: Text [ visible = { classroot.failed }, fontSize = 15,
                textColor = { provided("theme").faint }, wrap = true, width = 100%,
                text = { classroot.trouble + classroot.reason } ],
            doc: Markdown [ visible = { classroot.loaded }, width = 100%, lineHeight = 1.35,
                bodyColor = { provided("theme").lead }, dark = { app.isDark },
                text = { classroot.markdown } ],

            // the air between the document and the footer is the footer's own, at
            // this use site only: 86 is the 64 the design asks for plus the
            // column's own 22 of stack spacing, which lands above it either way

            Footer [ padding = [86, 0, 36, 0] ]
            ]
        ]
    ]


/*
## The palette

Two `theme` records — the language's own notation for a token set, so the
tokens are declarations rather than a JavaScript object a `{ }` body happens to
hold. The App spreads one of them over a library preset (below), which is what
lets the few library widgets on the page — a `Card`, a `Segmented`, a
`Tooltip` — find every token they read without a second record bolted onto
their subtree.

`Ink` is the dark page, `Paper` the light one: the same roles re-chosen for
paper rather than inverted.
*/

theme Ink [
    // text, brightest to faintest

    text = #E7EEF2, lead = #9DB0BC, muted = #8A9BA6, dim = #6A7C89, faint = #556673,

    // the blue accent and its interaction shades; links brighten past accent
    // (hover), then flash brighter still at the press — the same direction
    // FootLink's teal takes, so every text link answers a tap the same way

    accent = #4C8DFF, accentHover = #63A0FF, accentPress = #3D7BE0, linkHover = #7FB0FF, linkPress = #B2CFFF,

    // the teal second accent; the brand gradient runs accent → accentSecondary

    accentSecondary = #37E0C8, accentSecondaryPress = #9BF2E4,
    brand = gradient("90deg", #4C8DFF, #37E0C8),

    // the sequence badges' disc — true white, not the off-white text tone:
    // a solid counter can take full white where text-on-dark cannot

    badge = #FFFFFF,

    // grounds, darkest to lightest, and their interaction shades — `ink`
    // (both records) is also the page colour index.html paints before mount

    ink = #0B141B, panel = #0C1620, press = #0C1922, surfaceDeep = #0D1922,
    surface = #101E28, hover = #162A36, pillHover = #1C2D39, pillPress = #24384A,

    // rules and strokes. The rule drawn over a screenshot's edge is
    // translucent (#RRGGBBAA — the alpha is the low byte), so it reads on
    // light and dark pictures alike

    plateEdge = #FFFFFF2E,
    line = #263D4C, panelLine = #1E313C, revertLine = #26414F, pointRule = #345B84,
    grid = #172A38,

    // the error strip, the demo stage, and the overlay grounds

    errBg = #2A1518, errBar = #E0525E, errText = #F2A0A6,
    stage = #151A22, scrim = #0B141BB3, shade = #00000066
    ]


theme Paper [
    text = #14202A, lead = #3A4A57, muted = #5A6B78, dim = #72838F, faint = #8A99A4,
    accent = #2F6BDE, accentHover = #1F58C4, accentPress = #1A4AA6, linkHover = #1F58C4, linkPress = #173F8C,
    accentSecondary = #0FA38E, accentSecondaryPress = #0B7F6F,
    brand = gradient("90deg", #2F6BDE, #0FA38E),

    // the sequence discs invert on paper: a dark disc under a light digit

    badge = #14202A,
    ink = #F6F9FC, panel = #FFFFFF, press = #E9EEF2, surfaceDeep = #EEF2F5,
    surface = #FFFFFF, hover = #EEF3F7, pillHover = #E8EEF3, pillPress = #DCE4EB,
    plateEdge = #14202A2E,
    line = #D6DEE5, panelLine = #E1E7EC, revertLine = #D0D9E0, pointRule = #9DB6D6,

    // blueprint lines: a pale drafting blue, the light page's own paper

    grid = #D5E3F5,
    errBg = #FCEDEE, errBar = #D9434F, errText = #A42A34,

    // the demo stage is paper too: the demos read the page's appearance

    stage = #EDF1F5, scrim = #0B141B66, shade = #00000033
    ]


/*
## The page

The palette lives once as a provided `theme` token record — every semantic
type class defaults its color to `{ provided("theme").… }`, so the whole page reskins
from this one place. The scroller fills the viewport and scrolls its taller
content natively; the chrome further down (header, cursor) are its
**siblings**, not its children, so they never move.
*/

// THE HEADLINE LIVES HERE, ONCE. The hero reads it, and `appName` carries it
// into `document.title` at boot (runtime/src/boot.ts — "the ONE place that
// mapping lives"), which is also what the crawl extraction reports as the
// page's title and what the bake stamps into index.html's <title> and social
// cards. Stating it anywhere else would be a copy that only crawlers and LLMs
// ever read — the runtime overwrites document.title at boot, so a visitor
// would never see it drift. Exactly the readers this page is for.

App [ tagline: string = "a UI language for you and your AI",
    edges = cover,
    revealInset = 80,
    appName = { "Declare — " + app.tagline },

    // THIS PAGE IS DARK, whatever the machine is set to (DT, 2026-09-20). The
    // site's own presentation is dark — the shots, the video, the blueprint
    // grid and the plate shadows are all composed for it — so a light system
    // no longer decides how a first-time reader sees it. The header's switch
    // still flips to light and from then on the choice holds. Every colour
    // below reads the theme, so the whole page (and the hosted Architecture
    // article, through its island) follows.

    appearance: string = "dark",
    isDark: boolean = { app.appearance == "dark" },

    // the page's own record spread over the library preset in the same mode:
    // the page's tokens win, and the widgets the page borrows find the rest

    theme = { app.isDark ? ({ ...SanFranciscoDark, ...Ink }) : ({ ...SanFrancisco, ...Paper }) },
    fill = { provided("theme").ink },
    fontFamily = { app.uiFace }, fontSize = 17, fontWeight = normal,
    textColor = { provided("theme").text },

    // in-app location: "home" (the scrolling page), "why" (the mini-article),
    // "language" (the one-file core doc, rendered in-app), or "getstarted" (the
    // quick-start guide, rendered the same way) — the app’s slice of the
    // URL (docs/system-design/location.md). `location` is a BUILT-IN App attribute;
    // setting it here declares the INITIAL, which is the default: the host shows a
    // clean URL at "home" and `#why`/`#language` elsewhere, seeds it from a deep
    // link before first paint, and walks it on back/forward — no hash wiring in app
    // code. Every `shows` below derives from it; the header pills write it.
    // Nothing here returns the page to the top on a destination change either:
    // an anchorless follow lands its destination at the start, and an anchored
    // one is measured-reveal'd — both the runtime's, neither the app's.

    location = "home",

    /* No link-routing code, and none is wanted. Every href in the rendered
       Markdown below — the FAQ's, the guide's, the language doc's — routes
       through the runtime's own default: a "#name" follows in-app (destination
       or anchor alike, measured-reveal and all), anything else leaves through
       `navigate` (location.md §0.5). */
    // the page’s textual material, fetched DIRECTLY as text (format = "text"):
    // the core doc itself (docs/declare.md — up the tree, but still the
    // deployed repo’s own material), the getting-started guide, and the FAQ.
    // The authored .md files carry assembler markers as HTML comments, which
    // Markdown drops — so there is no JSON-wrap projection beside this file,
    // and a drifted copy is impossible because there is no copy. stats.json
    // stays JSON (line metrics computed by the prewarm pass, so the figures
    // below can never go stale).

    lang: DataSource [ url = "../../docs/declare.md", format = "text" ],
    stats: DataSource [ url = "stats.json" ],
    guide: DataSource [ url = "getstarted.md", format = "text" ],
    faq: DataSource [ url = "declare-faq.md", format = "text" ],

    // the Why article and the note that follows it — two documents because they
    // are two registers, and beside this file rather than inside it, like every
    // other piece of prose the site serves

    why: DataSource [ url = "why.md", format = "text" ],
    story: DataSource [ url = "why-story.md", format = "text" ],
    onInit() { this.lang.fetch(); this.stats.fetch(); this.guide.fetch(); this.faq.fetch()
               this.why.fetch(); this.story.fetch() },

    // `liveCard`/`liveSource` name the demo an inline editor is republishing
    // and its current text — the host recompiles that pair live. Editing the
    // whole page is `?viewer=edit`, which is the viewer's job, not this one's.

    liveCard: string = "",
    liveSource: string = "",

    // the site nav — ONE list of destinations, shared by the header pills (wide)
    // and the mobile sheet (narrow), so a link added here appears in both and the
    // two can never drift. An entry is a `label`, a `link` — "#name" follows
    // in-app, anything else leaves through `navigate`, and the runtime decides
    // which from the reference itself — and its `children`. `navSource` is the
    // Dataset both surfaces replicate from; `menuOpen` is the sheet's toggle.

    // The applications ride under one `Apps` entry. Standing alone in a nav row,
    // "Calendar", "Desktop" and "Tracker" name nothing a first-time reader can
    // act on — and beside a GitHub link two of them read as something else
    // entirely (a desktop build; an issue tracker). The parent supplies the
    // category the labels can't, and lands on §01, which is the page that
    // actually answers what they are: three shots, each with its line count and
    // wire size. The children keep their direct doors in the sheet, where there
    // is room for both.

    navItems: array = { [
        ({ label: "Getting Started", link: "#getstarted", children: [] }),
        ({ label: "Why Declare",     link: "#why",        children: [] }),
        ({ label: "FAQ",             link: "#faq",        children: [] }),
        ({ label: "Architecture",    link: "#architecture", children: [] }),
        // "#apps" is a BARE ANCHOR (§01's section carries `anchor = "apps"`):
        // the runtime derives its destination and lands on it — no section
        // machinery here, and the reference survives the section moving.
        ({ label: "Apps", link: "#apps", children: [
            ({ label: "Calendar", link: "apps/calendar/calendar.declare", children: [] }),
            ({ label: "Desktop",  link: "apps/desktop/desktop.declare",  children: [] }),
            ({ label: "Tracker",  link: "apps/tracker/tracker.declare",  children: [] }),
            ({ label: "Weather",  link: "apps/weather/weather.declare",  children: [] }),
            ({ label: "Market Map", link: "apps/marketmap/marketmap.declare", children: [] }),
            ({ label: "Birds",    link: "apps/birds/birds.declare",      children: [] }),
            ] }),
        ({ label: "Docs",     link: "apps/docs/docs.declare", children: [] }),
        // last, and the only item that leaves the site — an off-site link placed
        // mid-row would split the applications from the Docs they belong beside
        ({ label: "GitHub",   link: "https://github.com/davidtemkin/declarelang", children: [] }),
        ] },

    navSource: Dataset [ contents = { { items: app.navItems } } ],
    menuOpen: boolean = false,

    // the Apps dropdown (wide rendition): open state + the gesture-time anchor,
    // and its rows DERIVED from the one navItems list (no second list to drift)

    appsOpen: boolean = false,
    appsX: number = 0,
    appsY: number = 0,
    appsSource: Dataset [ contents = { { items: (app.navItems.find((i) => i.label == "Apps") || ({ children: [] })).children } } ],

    // the content measure and the centering gutter — the one geometry every
    // aligned edge on the page (column, wordmark, nav) derives from

    measure: number = 1120,
    gutter: number = { Math.max(24, (app.width - app.measure) / 2) },
    heroFont: number = { app.width < 700 ? 44 : 60 },

    // the site's heading weight — every Heading here, and the hosted
    // Architecture article's titles through the island bridge. Avenir Next runs
    // Regular 400, Medium 500, Demi Bold 600, Bold 700, Heavy 900; heavier
    // weights want looser tracking, so the tracking derives from the weight

    headWeight: number = 600,
    headTrack: number = { app.headWeight >= 900 ? -0.006 : app.headWeight >= 700 ? -0.014 : -0.02 },

    // the UI face — the site's Helvetica (fonts.declare), switchable to Avenir
    // from the type tuner while that is assessed

    faceName: string = "helvetica",
    uiFace: string[] = { app.faceName == "avenir" ? Faces.avenir : Faces.helvetica },

    // the front page's content — carried by the PAGE's own scroll (the App
    // scrolls by default, and its scroller is the browser's): taller than the
    // window, so the document scrolls, with the full native gesture tier

    scroller: View [ width = { app.width }, shows = "home",

        // full-bleed blueprint behind the column, clamped to the page's full
        // extent (page.y + page.height), so the paper runs to the very bottom
        // of the scroll range and no further — sizing to height alone left a
        // 96px paperless band at the bottom. Reading the page keeps this out of
        // the page's own auto-extent, so no cycle.

        grid: GridBg [ bottom = { parent.page.y + parent.page.height } ],

        // the centered content column: a fixed measure, and a VERTICAL FLOW of
        // sections — each auto-sizes, the page stacks them.
        //
        // `selectable` once, here, the way the Why and DocView columns say it:
        // this column is COPY, so every text leaf under it reads the provision
        // and the column is one selection surface (a drag can cross from a
        // heading into the paragraph under it). The controls inside it — a
        // Button's caption, a SourcePanel's chip and Revert, the links — each
        // say `selectable = false` where they stand.

        page: View [ width = { Math.min(app.measure, app.width - 48) }, selectable = true,
            x = { app.gutter }, y = 96,
            layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = { parent.width < 660 ? 72 : 112 } ],

            /* ### Hero — the name, the claim, and the two doors in
               (GitHub, or edit this page’s own source) */

            hero: View [ width = 100%,
                layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 24 ],
                top: View [ width = 100%,
                    layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 26 ],

                    // the title: product name in the blue→teal gradient, the claim on the
                    // next line. One rendition at every width — no wide/narrow split to keep in sync.

                    headline: View [ width = 100%,
                        layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 2 ],
                        Heading [ fontSize = { app.heroFont },
                            textFill = { provided("theme").brand }, text = "Declare" ],
                        Heading [ fontSize = { app.heroFont }, width = 100%,
                            text = { "is " + app.tagline + "." } ]
                        ],
                    paras: View [ width = 100%,
                        layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 12 ],
                        HTMLText [ width = { Math.min(680, parent.width) }, fontSize = 19, lineHeight = 1.2, bodyColor = { provided("theme").lead },
                            html = "Just as SQL is a domain-specific language for database queries, <span class='g'>Declare</span> is a DSL for user interfaces. Its whole surface fits in your head — and inside an LLM’s context window." ],

                        // The page's most arresting fact, and the only claim on it a
                        // visitor can falsify in their own browser in three clicks —
                        // so it reads as a statement, not a caption under the one above.

                        Lead [ width = { Math.min(680, parent.width) }, fontWeight = semibold,
                            textColor = { provided("theme").text },
                            text = "This page is written in it. By an LLM." ]
                        ]
                    ],

                // the two doors in, side by side with room between them

                learn: View [ width = 100%,
                    layout: ResponsiveLayout [ plan = { [({ from: 600, flow: "row", gap: 32 }), ({ from: 0, flow: "stack", gap: 8 })] } ],
                    why: SmallLink [ fontSize = 15, text = "Why a new language, now? →", link = "#why" ],
                    onefile: SmallLink [ fontSize = 15, text = "The whole language, in one file →", link = "#language" ]
                    ]
                ],

            /* ### 01 — Three applications. The calendar leads at full width; the
               tracker and the desktop share the row beneath, and stack on a
               phone. Each numbered section fades in on a Spring the moment it
               scrolls into view — a reveal declared as a reactive target, not
               scheduled. Every figure below is read from `stats.json`, which the
               prewarm pass regenerates from the real production builds, so no
               number here can drift from the tree. */

            // `anchor = "apps"` — the §01 section IS the `#apps` reference's
            // target, so the reference survives the section moving; and
            // App.revealInset clears the fixed header on arrival

            apps: ScrollReveal [ anchor = "apps",

                // 72, not the page's usual 40: the three applications are three
                // separate things, and the calendar's own caption sits inside its
                // row — too little air here and the button reads as the tracker's

                layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 72 ],

                SectionHead [ num = "01", head = "Three applications, written in Declare.",
                    lead = "Complete programs — open one, read it end to end, and edit it live in the browser you're using." ],

                /* The calendar takes its own row: the pitch on the LEFT where the
                   eye starts, the plate on the right at half the column — the same
                   size as the two beneath, so all three pictures agree. Below 860
                   the row stacks, and because the words come first in declaration
                   order the stack reads pitch-then-picture too.

                   `hot` is the row's one hover fact: the title lights the plate and
                   the plate lights the title, each derived from the other's raw
                   `hovered`, never from its styling — so they answer together with
                   no cycle. */

                cal: View [ width = 100%,
                    hot: boolean = { this.plate.hovered || this.tell.head.name.hovered || this.tell.head.seq.hovered },
                    layout: ResponsiveLayout [ plan = { [({ from: 860, flow: "row", gap: 40, share: ({ tell: 50, plate: 50 }) }),
                                                         ({ from: 0, flow: "stack", gap: 26, share: ({ tell: 100, plate: 100 }) })] } ],

                    tell: View [ layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 18 ],

                        // the row's hover fact, handed in ONCE at the use site —
                        // the badge and the name read it as `classroot.hot`
                        // rather than each climbing three parents to find it

                        head: CardHead [ num = "1", title = "Declare Calendar",
                            url = "apps/calendar/calendar.declare",
                            hot = { parent.parent.hot } ],

                        Lead [ fontSize = 17, width = 100%,
                            text = "It’s the kind of experience you’ll see in the best native apps — a view doesn’t switch so much as become the next one, where motion carries meaning, and everything is fast and interruptible." ],

                        // The clip beside this shows a detail panel opening and an
                        // event being dragged to another day — operations, not
                        // motion. This line names that, because it is the clip's
                        // strongest beat and nothing else in the column claims it:
                        // it answers the doubt a video invites — is this the thing,
                        // or a recording of it — with what the drag commits.

                        Lead [ fontSize = 17, width = 100%,
                            text = "It runs on real data: open an event, drag it to another day, and it’s moved." ],

                        // computed, not claimed — stats.json, regenerated from the
                        // real production build on every prewarm

                        stat: View [ width = 100%, height = { this.contentHeight },
                            layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = x, spacing = 20 ],

                            // CODE lines, and the note says so. The file is longer
                            // than this — open it and you count ~800 — so a bare
                            // "lines of Declare" reads as caught out rather than
                            // modest. Naming the comment lines is what makes the
                            // smaller number legible as the honest one. Each figure
                            // is its own read: a helper taking the record would put
                            // the reads inside a body the extractor cannot see.

                            MiniMetric [ value = { "" + (app.stats.read(["calendar", "code"]) ?? "—") },
                                note = { "lines of code, plus "
                                    + (app.stats.read(["calendar", "comment"]) ?? "—")
                                    + " of comments — four views, zoom, drag and edit" } ],
                            MiniMetric [ value = { app.stats.read(["calendar", "wireGzip"]) ? Math.round(app.stats.read(["calendar", "wireGzip"]) / 1024) + " KB" : "—" },
                                note = "over the wire, gzipped — app (structure, logic, styling) and runtime" ],
                            MiniMetric [ value = "0",
                                note = "lines written by hand — an LLM wrote it" ]
                            ],

                        actions: View [ width = 100%,
                            layout: ResponsiveLayout [ plan = { [({ from: 420, flow: "row", gap: 18, align: "center" }), ({ from: 0, flow: "stack", gap: 14 })] } ],

                            // PRIMARY: the accent fill. This is the page's one
                            // call to action — running the thing the section has
                            // just described — and it reads as the button it is
                            // rather than as another quiet surface.

                            SiteButton [ width = 240, primary = true, label = "Run Declare Calendar →",
                                link = "apps/calendar/calendar.declare" ],

                            // the quieter way in, centred on the button while
                            // the two share a row (the row tier's align)

                            SmallLink [ text = "View & edit source →",
                                link = "apps/calendar/calendar.declare?viewer" ]
                            ]
                        ],

                    // the one claim a still cannot make: four views that BECOME each
                    // other. It runs once the section has revealed — `parent.parent`
                    // is the apps band, whose opacity is that reveal — so a visitor
                    // who never scrolls here never decodes a frame.

                    plate: Shot [ src = { app.isDark ? "shots/calendar-dark.webp" : "shots/calendar-light.webp" }, link = "apps/calendar/calendar.declare",
                        // Its last frame IS its first, so the loop has no seam to
                        // notice. What it shows is the app being USED — a detail
                        // panel opening on a real record, an event dragged to
                        // another day — not a view transforming for its own sake.
                        // shots/video/ holds the clip and the script that cuts it

                        motion = { app.isDark ? "shots/video/calendar-dark.mp4" : "shots/video/calendar-light.mp4" },
                        live = { parent.parent.opacity > 0.9 },
                        hot = { this.parent.tell.head.name.hovered } ]
                    ],

                // the pair, with a wider gutter than the calendar row's — two
                // whole applications side by side need more air between them than
                // a picture needs from its own caption

                pair: View [ width = 100%,
                    layout: ResponsiveLayout [ plan = { [({ from: 720, flow: "row", gap: 56, share: ({ trk: 50, dsk: 50 }) }),
                                                         ({ from: 0, flow: "stack", gap: 56, share: ({ trk: 100, dsk: 100 }) })] } ],

                    trk: AppCard [ title = "Declare Tracker", num = "2",
                        shot   = { app.isDark ? "shots/tracker-dark.webp" : "shots/tracker-light.webp" },
                        url    = "apps/tracker/tracker.declare",
                        srcUrl = "apps/tracker/tracker.declare?viewer",
                        blurb = "1,000,000 issues · search as you type · edit in place, with undo · not one line of virtualization code",

                        // the wire figure is the production BUILD — app, runtime and
                        // library slices. The tracker's 3 MB issues.json is fetched by a
                        // DataSource at run time and is not in it, so the line says so:
                        // "before its data" is the difference between a small app and a
                        // small download.

                        figures = { hpLines(app.stats.read(["tracker","total"]), app.stats.read(["tracker","code"]), app.stats.read(["tracker","comment"]))
                              + hpWire(app.stats.read(["tracker","wireGzip"])) + ", before its data"
                              + " · no dependencies · the same brief in idiomatic React: 2,703 lines, 9 dependencies" } ],

                    dsk: AppCard [ title = "Declare Desktop", num = "3",
                        shot   = { app.isDark ? "shots/desktop-dark.webp" : "shots/desktop-light.webp" },
                        url    = "apps/desktop/desktop.declare",
                        srcUrl = "apps/desktop/desktop.declare?viewer",
                        blurb = "Windows that drag, resize, layer, and minimize into live dock thumbnails · a magnifying dock · menus that follow focus · the calendar running inside one of them",
                        figures = { hpLines(app.stats.read(["desktop","total"]), app.stats.read(["desktop","code"]), app.stats.read(["desktop","comment"]))
                              + hpWire(app.stats.read(["desktop","wireGzip"])) + " · every pixel drawn from shapes, no image assets" } ]
                    ]
                ],

            /* ### 02–04 — one `DemoSection` each. These three are the language's
               genuinely new ideas — a constraint, replication over data, and a
               spring — one each, nothing else. */

            s3: DemoSection [ num = "02", head = "Declare it. It runs. It stays true.",
                lead = "A binding isn’t a callback you remember to re-run — it’s a standing relationship the runtime keeps true, re-derived the moment its inputs change.",
                caption = "Three bindings track one value: the big number, the bar’s width, and its color. Click to bump v — all three update together, and the color crosses cool to warm at the halfway mark.",
                demo = "reactivity" ],

            s4: DemoSection [ num = "03", head = "Declare the shape, not the loop.",
                lead = "A view can be built from data instead of laid out by hand — the shape written once, the data deciding how many there are and what each one says.",
                caption = "One Bar, instanced once per row of the data. Edit a number or add a row and the list rebuilds itself — no loop, no keys. And Bar is auto-included: no import, no definition, just use it.",
                demo = "components" ],

            s5: DemoSection [ num = "04", head = "Motion you declare, not schedule.",
                lead = "You say where a thing belongs; a Spring finds the path there and settles by physics — motion you declare, not a timeline you sequence.",
                caption = "Click to toggle the ball’s target between two positions. The Spring follows and settles by physics — no timeline, no frames driven by hand.",
                demo = "spring" ],

            /* ### The feedback loop — the page's argument for WHY the language is
               shaped this way, placed where it can land: after three tiny programs
               the reader has just read, not before.

               Four beats of one loop — context, correction, inspection, proof —
               each a `Point`, the accent-ruled title and body. This is the only
               place on the page that wears it. */

            loop: ScrollReveal [
                layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 36 ],

                head: View [ width = 100%,
                    layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 20 ],

                    BannerHead [ fontSize = { app.width < 700 ? 24 : 30 }, measure = 880,
                        text = "Your AI can write the code. Can it tell when it’s wrong?" ],

                    // The answer to the head's question, and the bridge to the four
                    // beats: catch (question), fix (correct), finish (prove) — the
                    // grid previewed without being listed, and "finish" last, where
                    // the sentence's weight falls. The context beat is "started".

                    BannerLead [ fontSize = 17, measure = 620,
                        text = "In Declare, yes. A small language gets it started; these four tools let it catch its mistakes, fix them, and finish." ]
                    ],

                // Sized to the TEXT rather than to the page, and centered: at the
                // full 1120 measure two 50% shares strand a dead gutter between
                // columns whose text runs far shorter, and the pair stop reading
                // as one block.
                //
                // Paired ROWS, not two columns. The four are one ordered loop, so
                // they read across in the order they happen — and a pair per row
                // is what keeps two accent rules on one line. Sliced into columns
                // instead, unequal text staggers the rules and the grid reads
                // broken.
                //
                // Each beat sits in a bare cell because the ResponsiveLayout
                // sizes its own children and a Point carries a width of its own
                // (R4 says so, by name). The cell takes the share; the Point
                // fills the cell.

                beats: View [ width = { Math.min(880, parent.width) }, x = center,
                    layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 32 ],

                    first: View [ width = 100%,
                        layout: ResponsiveLayout [ plan = { [({ from: 760, flow: "row", gap: 56, share: ({ a: 50, b: 50 }) }),
                                                             ({ from: 0, flow: "stack", gap: 32, share: ({ a: 100, b: 100 }) })] } ],
                        a: View [ Point [ width = 100%, ptitle = "Start with context",
                                body = "One installable skill: the language, the library, the conventions, the working loop — nothing to reconstruct from a repository. And declare-help answers any name or error code on demand." ]
                            ],
                        b: View [ Point [ width = 100%, ptitle = "Correct, don’t guess",
                                body = "Every diagnostic names the broken rule and the valid rewrite. Try it: change textColor to color in the first panel above and read what comes back." ]
                            ]
                        ],

                    second: View [ width = 100%,
                        layout: ResponsiveLayout [ plan = { [({ from: 760, flow: "row", gap: 56, share: ({ a: 50, b: 50 }) }),
                                                             ({ from: 0, flow: "stack", gap: 32, share: ({ a: 100, b: 100 }) })] } ],
                        a: View [ Point [ width = 100%, ptitle = "Question the result",
                                body = "The Inspector can tell you or an agent why a live value is what it is, what it depends on, and what happens if you change it — live, in the running program, not guessed from source." ]
                            ],
                        b: View [ Point [ width = 100%, ptitle = "Prove the change",
                                body = "A formal six-rung ladder, from parse to pixels, is how an app is shown to be correct. Your AI climbs it one rung at a time, and every rung reports what it proved — and what it cannot see." ]
                            ]
                        ]
                    ],

                // The close is the proof already on the page — the calendar's own
                // "0 lines written by hand" — not a restatement of the four beats.
                // No door: the FAQ is one click away in the header on every view.

                close: BannerLead [ fontSize = 17, measure = 760,
                    text = "The calendar above was built this way: a person directed, an AI wrote every line, and the compiler kept it honest." ]
                ],

            /* ### The closing line — centered between two accent rules. Two
               beats, one per half of the headline: the language you can know
               whole, and the toolchain that makes what comes back trustworthy.
               The second beat answers the loop section above it, which is why
               the line is not about size twice. */

            closer: ScrollReveal [
                layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 30 ],
                ruleTop: BandRule [ ],
                BannerHead [ fontSize = { app.width < 700 ? 26 : 38 }, measure = 900,
                    text = "Small enough to know. Strict enough to trust." ],
                ruleBot: BandRule [ ]
                ],

            /* ### The closing call to action — the primary blue button that
               leads into the getting-started page, centered above the footer */

            /* the page flow’s 112px would sit both above and below this one
               short row — the button pulls up into the gap (negative y, no
               clip) and the zero-height container lets the footer follow
               closer: ~88px above, ~90px below instead of 112/112 */

            cta: View [ width = 100%, height = 0,
                SiteButton [ width = 260, x = center, y = -24, label = "Get Started →", link = "#getstarted" ]
                ],

            /* ### Footer */

            // The footer carries its own bottom margin. A spacer view here would
            // also take the page flow's 112px spacing above it, which is 140px of
            // dead band under the footer for 28px of wanted air.

            footer: Footer [ ]
            ]
        ],

    /* ## The "Why" view

       An in-app view (`app.location == "why"`) wearing the same skin as the page —
       theme, graph-paper grid, and the header renders here too. The host mirrors
       `app.location` to the URL fragment (#why), so it deep-links and honours the
       back button — for free, from the one built-in binding. The article itself is
       prose in `"""` blocks: HTML collapses the line breaks, so the source wraps
       and the page flows. */

    whyView: View [ shows = "why", width = { app.width }, fill = { provided("theme").ink },
        grid: GridBg [ bottom = { parent.body.y + parent.body.height } ],
        body: View [ y = 56, width = { app.width }, selectable = true,
            col: View [ width = { Math.min(720, app.width - 48) }, x = center, y = 52,

                // the air under the footer is the column's own bottom inset —
                // an unset height counts it in, so the page's scroll range ends
                // where the paper does. (A tail view in the flow took the
                // layout's own 22 above it as well; 62 is that pair.)

                padding = [0, 0, 62, 0],
                layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 22 ],
                Marker [ text = "WHY DECLARE" ],

                // The essay is a document, so it arrives as one: why.md, fetched
                // as text and flowed by the Markdown component — the same
                // pipeline the FAQ, the guide and the language doc use.

                essay: Markdown [ visible = { app.why.loaded }, width = 100%, lineHeight = 1.35,
                    bodyColor = { provided("theme").lead }, dark = { app.isDark },
                    linkColor = { provided("theme").accent },
                    text = { app.why.value || "" } ],
                wait: Text [ visible = { app.why.loading }, fontSize = 15,
                    textColor = { provided("theme").faint }, text = "Loading…" ],
                oops: Text [ visible = { app.why.failed }, fontSize = 15, wrap = true, width = 100%,
                    textColor = { provided("theme").faint },
                    text = { "The essay did not load — " + (app.why.error || "") } ],

                /* ### Where this came from

                   A second movement, not a postscript: the essay above answers
                   "why a new language, now?", and this answers "where did this
                   one come from?" — a different question, in the first person.
                   Marked as its own section (breath, hairline, eyebrow) rather
                   than run on from the last paragraph, so the essay's closing
                   line still reads as an ending.

                   Its own Markdown instance, a step smaller and in the muted
                   tone: quieter than the argument it follows. One document
                   carries one register, so this is two documents. */

                breath: View [ width = 8, height = 18 ],

                // `anchor = "story"` — "#story" reaches this note from anywhere
                // (the FAQ's cross-reference), the destination derived, the
                // landing measured-reveal'd, the header cleared by revealInset

                rule:   Divider [ anchor = "story" ],
                Marker [ text = "WHERE THIS CAME FROM" ],

                story: Markdown [ visible = { app.story.loaded }, width = 100%,
                    fontSize = 16, lineHeight = 1.35,
                    bodyColor = { provided("theme").muted }, dark = { app.isDark },
                    linkColor = { provided("theme").accent },
                    text = { app.story.value || "" } ],

                // the signature keeps its own quieter tone, a step below the
                // note's — one line of chrome, not a paragraph of it

                sig: Text [ fontSize = 16, italic = true, width = 100%,
                    textColor = { provided("theme").dim }, text = "— David Temkin" ],

                Footer [ padding = [86, 0, 36, 0] ]
                ]
            ]
        ],

    /* ## The "Language" view

       The one-file core doc (docs/declare.md), rendered in-app by the native
       Markdown component — same skin and same location machinery as the Why
       article, so it deep-links (#language) and is crawled into the static
       document like any other reachable location. The text arrives as the
       app's own material — docs/declare.md fetched directly, format = "text":
       loading and failure are lifecycle states the view derives from, never
       a blank. */

    languageView: DocView [ shows = "language", eyebrow = "THE LANGUAGE",
        waiting = "Loading the language…",
        trouble = "The language file did not load — ",
        loading = { app.lang.loading }, failed = { app.lang.failed }, loaded = { app.lang.loaded },
        reason = { app.lang.error || "" }, markdown = { app.lang.value || "" } ],

    /* ## The "Get Started" view

       The getting-started guide (app.location == "getstarted"), rendered in-app
       with the same skin and Markdown pipeline as the Language view: the copy is
       getstarted.md itself (an authored page, its command blocks generated), so
       it deep-links (#getstarted), honours the back button, and is crawled into
       the static document like any other reachable location. Loading and failure
       are lifecycle states the view derives from, never a blank. */

    getStartedView: DocView [ shows = "getstarted", eyebrow = "GETTING STARTED",
        waiting = "Loading the guide…",
        trouble = "The guide did not load — ",
        loading = { app.guide.loading }, failed = { app.guide.failed }, loaded = { app.guide.loaded },
        reason = { app.guide.error || "" }, markdown = { app.guide.value || "" } ],

    /* ## The FAQ view — the Get Started pattern at #faq */

    faqView: DocView [ shows = "faq", eyebrow = "FAQ",
        waiting = "Loading…",
        trouble = "The FAQ did not load — ",
        loading = { app.faq.loading }, failed = { app.faq.failed }, loaded = { app.faq.loaded },
        reason = { app.faq.error || "" }, markdown = { app.faq.value || "" } ],

    /* ## The Architecture view — a whole program, hosted

       Not an article in this file: a separate Declare program
       (apps/architecture), mounted by an AppIsland under the header. The
       tenant exposes its height and the island takes it, so the homepage's page lengthens and its own scroll carries
       the article. The program is attached only while this view shows —
       `program = ""` detaches it — so the homepage pays nothing for it until
       someone opens it. The island provides it the site's appearance and type. */

    architectureView: View [ shows = "architecture", width = { app.width },
        fill = { provided("theme").ink },

        // the same paper as Why and the FAQ, behind a tenant that paints no
        // ground of its own — following the scroll, clamped to the island

        grid: GridBg [ bottom = { parent.island.y + parent.island.height } ],

        island: AppIsland [ y = { 56 + app.safeTop }, width = { app.width },
            program = { app.location == "architecture" ? "../../architecture/architecture" : "" },

            // the site's appearance and type, provided to the tenant by name —
            // it reads each with hostProvided(name, default)

            provides = ["hosted", "dark", "face", "headWeight"],
            hosted: boolean = true,
            dark: boolean = { app.isDark },
            face: string = { app.faceName },
            headWeight: number = { app.headWeight },

            // the tenant's own full height, as it exposes it: the island takes it, so the homepage's page grows to hold the whole
            // article and its own scroll carries it, as with Why and the FAQ

            height = { Math.max(this.exposed("docH", 0), app.height - 56 - app.safeTop) }
            ]
        ],

    /* ## Fixed chrome

       `ignoreScroll` — the scroll carries everyone but these; they ride the
       window while the page's own scroll carries the content: the top bar, the
       type tuner, the pointer dot, the mobile nav sheet and the Apps dropdown. */

    // the bar is ALWAYS present, stuck to the top: a first-time visitor sees
    // the whole map without having to scroll for it

    header: View [ ignoreScroll = true, width = { app.width }, height = { 56 + app.safeTop }, fill = { provided("theme").surface },

        // the rule under the header is the header's own bottom border — one
        // side of a four-sided stroke, not a hairline child to keep in step

        stroke = { [null, null, stroke(1, provided("theme").line), null] },

        /* The header row, gutter to gutter. ONE ResponsiveLayout decides whether
           the pill row fits, and `share: 0` IS the hiding — so there is no width
           test written by hand and no `visible` to keep in step with a number.
           Above the threshold the plan drops the kebab; below it the plan drops
           the pills and the kebab takes over, the whole list moving to the sheet.
           Nothing is ever dropped from the list itself, unlike a progressively
           trimmed pill row.

           The threshold is the row's OWN width, not the window's (a plan watches
           its view), and it is measured: the wordmark runs 53, the pill run 570
           and the appearance switch 34 — 657 in all, so 740 leaves 83 of air
           between the wordmark and the run. It is also the one number that moves
           when `navItems` gains an entry — which is why it is stated once.
           (Folding the three applications under a single `Apps` pill took 179
           out of the row; the Architecture entry has since put some back.)

           The Spacer is what right-aligns the run: it absorbs the slack between
           the wordmark and whatever follows. Shares fill; naturals align.

           Every item is centred on the bar's height, and the wordmark sits one
           pixel below centre, where its letters read level with the pills. */

        bar: View [ x = { app.gutter }, y = { app.safeTop }, height = 56,
            width = { app.width - app.gutter * 2 },
            layout: ResponsiveLayout [ gap = 0, align = center, plan = { [
                ({ from: 740, flow: "row", share: ({ kebab: 0 }), offset: ({ brand: 1 }) }),
                ({ from: 0,   flow: "row", share: ({ nav: 0 }),   offset: ({ brand: 1 }) })] } ],

            // wordmark — the product name in the brand gradient, in the hero's typeface

            brand: View [ height = 22,
                layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = x, spacing = 0 ],
                link = "#home",
                Display [ fontSize = 15, textFill = { provided("theme").brand }, text = "Declare" ]
                ],

            Spacer [ ],

            // primary nav — one NavLink per navItems entry; the padded pills carry
            // their own spacing

            nav: View [
                layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = x, spacing = 2 ],
                datapath = { app.navSource.value },
                NavLink [ datapath = :items[], label = { :label }, menu = { :children.length > 0 },
                    link = { :children.length > 0 ? "" : :link } ]
                ],

            // the appearance switch — one icon, two states: it shows the mode
            // you are in, and a press flips to the other. Until pressed, the
            // page tracks the system; after, it keeps what you chose.

            modeBtn: View [ width = 34, height = 33, cornerRadius = 8,
                fill = { pressed ? provided("theme").pillPress : hovered ? provided("theme").pillHover : null },
                tip = { app.isDark ? "Switch to light" : "Switch to dark" },
                onClick() { app.appearance = app.isDark ? "light" : "dark" },
                SunIcon [ x = center, y = center, iconSize = 16, visible = { !app.isDark },
                    ink = { parent.hovered ? provided("theme").text : provided("theme").muted } ],
                MoonIcon [ x = center, y = center, iconSize = 16, visible = { app.isDark },
                    ink = { parent.hovered ? provided("theme").text : provided("theme").muted } ]
                ],

            // the kebab — three stacked dots (the page's dot motif), the pill's own
            // press feedback, toggling the sheet

            kebab: View [ width = 34, height = 33, cornerRadius = 8,
                fill = { pressed ? provided("theme").pillPress : hovered ? provided("theme").pillHover : null },
                onClick()     { app.menuOpen = !app.menuOpen },
                d1: View [ x = center, y = 8,  width = 3, height = 3, cornerRadius = 1.5, fill = { parent.hovered || parent.pressed ? provided("theme").text : provided("theme").muted } ],
                d2: View [ x = center, y = 15, width = 3, height = 3, cornerRadius = 1.5, fill = { parent.hovered || parent.pressed ? provided("theme").text : provided("theme").muted } ],
                d3: View [ x = center, y = 22, width = 3, height = 3, cornerRadius = 1.5, fill = { parent.hovered || parent.pressed ? provided("theme").text : provided("theme").muted } ]
                ]
            ]
        ],

    // ── type tuner (experiment) ─────────────────────────────────────
    // a floating panel for assessing the face and the heading weight live;
    // it rides the viewport, bottom right, above everything else. Hidden while
    // the page is set in Helvetica — flip `showTuner` to bring it back.

    showTuner: boolean = false,

    // It is a `Card`: the library's themed surface, so the fill, the hairline and
    // the inset that stacks its contents all come from the preset below instead
    // of being spelled out here. The card takes the height its content needs,
    // padding counted in, so there is no arithmetic to keep in step.

    tuner: Card [ ignoreScroll = true, width = 300, x = { app.width - 320 }, y = { app.height - this.height - 20 },
        visible = { app.showTuner },

        // no theme of its own: the App's record already carries the library
        // preset, so the Card and its Segmenteds find every token they read

        cornerRadius = 12, padding = 14, shadow = { shadow(0, 10, 30, 0x00000040) },
        layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 10 ],
        Eyebrow [ fontSize = 11, textColor = { provided("theme").textMuted }, text = "type tuner · headings and face" ],
        Segmented [ width = 100%, height = 30,
            value = { "" + app.headWeight },
            choices = { [ ({ id: "600", label: "Demi 600" }), ({ id: "700", label: "Bold 700" }), ({ id: "900", label: "Heavy 900" }) ] },
            input(v: object) { app.headWeight = Number(v) }
            ],
        Segmented [ width = 100%, height = 30,
            value = { app.faceName },
            choices = { [ ({ id: "helvetica", label: "Helvetica" }), ({ id: "avenir", label: "Avenir Next" }) ] },
            input(v: object) { app.faceName = "" + v }
            ]
        ],

    // pointer dot — a 10px accent-2 dot in VIEWPORT space that SPRINGS after the
    // pointer (loose + low-mass = the slower, longer trail). Two position
    // Springs; the native cursor stays visible and leads, the dot follows.

    pointerDot: View [ ignoreScroll = true, width = 10, height = 10, cornerRadius = 5, fill = { provided("theme").accentSecondary }, opacity = 0.9,
        shadow = { shadow(0, 0, 12, provided("theme").accentSecondary) },

        // Decoration, not a target: the dot settles exactly under a resting
        // cursor and the hit chain is occlusion-correct, so without this line the
        // dot takes every hover and press the moment the mouse stops — a click
        // still resolves, but `hovered` and `pressed` styling go dead at rest.

        pointerEvents = "none",

        // Only for a HOVERING pointer (mouse / trackpad) — `app.hovering` is false
        // for touch, so the dot never appears on a phone, yet an iPad trackpad
        // (which reports a "mouse" pointer) still gets it. Also yields to the
        // native I-beam when the pointer is over an editable field.

        visible = { app.hovering && !app.pointerOverText },
        followX: Spring [ attribute = x, to = { app.pointerX - 5 },
            stiffness = 120, damping = 14, mass = 0.6, epsilon = 0.05 ],
        followY: Spring [ attribute = y, to = { app.pointerY - 5 },
            stiffness = 120, damping = 14, mass = 0.6, epsilon = 0.05 ]
        ],

    // the mobile nav sheet — the kebab's menu, above everything. A dim scrim below
    // the header catches an outside tap (and leaves the header + kebab lit, so the
    // kebab still toggles it shut); the top-right panel lists every destination as
    // a thumb-height row, replicated from the SAME navSource as the pills. Auto-hides
    // if the window is widened back past the breakpoint.

    navSheet: View [ ignoreScroll = true, visible = { app.menuOpen && app.header.bar.kebab.visible }, width = { app.width }, height = { app.height },
        scrim: View [ y = { 56 + app.safeTop }, width = 100%, height = { parent.height - 56 - app.safeTop }, fill = { provided("theme").scrim },
            onClick() { app.menuOpen = false }
            ],

        // The 6px all round is the panel's own `padding`, so the rows it holds
        // are its direct children and there is no inner view to carry the inset.
        // Its height is DERIVED and unwritten: both insets count toward an unset
        // height, so the panel is exactly its rows plus the air around them —
        // and rows and group children are no longer one uniform height, so
        // `navItems.length * 44` stated a relationship this sheet stopped having.

        panel: View [ x = { app.width - app.gutter - this.width }, y = { 62 + app.safeTop }, width = 236,
            cornerRadius = 12, padding = 6,
            fill = { provided("theme").surface }, shadow = { shadow(0, 12, 40, provided("theme").shade) },
            layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 0 ],
            datapath = { app.navSource.value },
            MobileNavGroup [ datapath = :items[] ]
            ]
        ],

    // the Apps dropdown — the pill row's own menu, the same rows as the sheet
    // (MobileNavRow), anchored under the Apps pill. A transparent catcher
    // below closes it on an outside tap; narrowing past the breakpoint hides
    // it with the pills it belongs to.

    appsMenu: View [ ignoreScroll = true, visible = { app.appsOpen && !app.header.bar.kebab.visible },
        width = { app.width }, height = { app.height },
        catcher: View [ width = 100%, height = 100%,
            onClick() { app.appsOpen = false }
            ],
        panel: View [ x = { app.appsX }, y = { app.appsY }, width = 190,
            cornerRadius = 12, padding = 6,
            fill = { provided("theme").surface }, shadow = { shadow(0, 12, 40, provided("theme").shade) },
            layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 0 ],
            datapath = { app.appsSource.value },
            MobileNavRow [ datapath = :items[], label = { :label }, link = { :link } ]
            ]
        ]
    ]
